 "text": "At 23, Alice Ball made a leprosy oil usable, then lost the credit. Swallowed it caused nausea, injected it hurt and barely absorbed. She turned its fatty acids into ethyl esters, then oils the body absorbs. She died at 24, her supervisor published the work without crediting her. A 1922 paper called it Ball's method. Lesson, a remedy only helps if patients can take it. Download RM to support livelihood tools."
